S2[52] = {
1: "Idea: L = 0.95 × c/(2f). c/(2 × 435×10⁶) = 0.345 m; × 0.95 = 0.327 m. Check: three times the frequency of the 2 m dipole, a third of its 0.977 m length. The usual mistake is to forget the end-effect factor and cut 5 % long.",
2: "Idea: a resonance above the target means the antenna is too short; lengthen by the fractional error. (150.2 − 145.8)/145.8 = 3.0 %, so lengthen by about 3 %. Check: length scales as 1/f. The usual mistake is to shorten it further because the SWR looked too high.",
3: "Idea: λ/4 = c/(4f). 299.79/868/4 = 8.63 cm. Check: 915 MHz gave 8.19 cm; 868 MHz is lower, so longer. The usual mistake is to apply a dipole's 0.95 factor to a whip over a small ground plane without measuring.",
4: "Idea: Δf = f v/c. 437×10⁶ × 5×10³/3×10⁸ = 7.28 kHz. Check: 10.5 kHz at 7.2 km/s scales to 7.28 kHz at 5 km/s. The usual mistake is to use the satellite's orbital speed instead of its radial component.",
5: "Idea: ΔTmin = Tsys/√(Bτ), so τ = (Tsys/ΔTmin)²/B. (100/0.005)² = 4×10⁸; τ = 4×10⁸/2.4×10⁶ = 167 s. Check: four times finer than 0.020 K needs sixteen times longer than 10 s, 160 s. The usual mistake is to scale the time linearly.",
6: "Idea: θ ≈ 70λ/D degrees. 70 × 0.211/3 = 4.9°. Check: 2.5 times the 1.2 m dish's diameter gives 2.5 times narrower than 12.3°. The usual mistake is to use the 1420 MHz frequency in place of the 21.1 cm wavelength.",
}
# ---- solutions for problems added to existing chapters (numbers continue the chapter's list)
SX = {
22: {7: "Idea: Ropt = (V − Vk)²/(2P) for each technology. Battery: 3.1²/4 = 2.40 Ω. GaN: 25²/4 = 156.3 Ω. The ratio is 65.0. Check: the ratio equals (25/3.1)², the squared ratio of the swings, independent of the power. The usual mistake is to compare the supply ratio, 28/3.4 = 8.2, instead of its square.",
     8: "Idea: bipolar fingers need ballast against thermal runaway and a bias that tracks VBE with temperature, which a MOSFET's negative temperature coefficient of current at high bias does not need. A CMOS stage instead worries about gate resistance per finger and the oxide's voltage limit. Check: Sections 44.3 and 42.4. The usual mistake is to copy a CMOS layout for an HBT without ballast resistors."},
28: {7: "Idea: F = Fmin + (4Rn/Z0)|Γs − Γopt|²/((1 − |Γs|²)|1 + Γopt|²). |Γs − Γopt| = 2 × 0.27 × sin 30° = 0.27, so |Γs − Γopt|² = 0.0729, 1 − |Γs|² = 0.927, and F = 1.109 + 0.16 × 0.0729/(0.927 × 0.803) = 1.125, 0.51 dB. Check: the same 0.0729 distance as the 50 Ω source gives about the same noise figure. The usual mistake is to think the right magnitude is half of the noise match; the angle matters as much."},
41: {11: "Idea: choose by the signal envelope, the power, and the frequency. The 433 MHz remote sends a constant envelope at low power, so a saturated class C or E stage from a single transistor is cheapest and most efficient. The 3.5 GHz macro cell sends OFDM with a high peak-to-average ratio at tens of watts, so a GaN Doherty with predistortion, Chapter 45. The 6.78 MHz charger delivers a constant sine to a varying coil load, so a class E or class D inverter, Chapter 43. Check: Section 41.12's list. The usual mistake is to pick one class for everything because it has the highest ideal efficiency."},
}
